# Finite model of hauntology as branching time (a model, not a claim about history).
# Worlds: 0 lost (a future that did not happen), 1 present, 2 haunting (a future that is still felt).
# Relation: from the present a lost future is reachable and a haunting future is reachable; a lost future leads nowhere.
kripke H = worlds 3 rel [(1,0), (1,2), (2,2)] val lost {0} present {1} haunting {2}
# Which worlds have a lost future one step away? Only the present.
claim worlds H "<>lost" = {1}
# Which worlds are haunted in every step? Only the haunting world, which is self-looping.
claim worlds H "[]haunting" = {2}
# Which worlds reach a haunting world in one step? The present and the haunting world itself.
claim worlds H "<>haunting" = {1,2}
# Note on the false claim (kept as found): "[]haunting" is also true at world 0, because world 0 has no successors, so every successor of 0 is haunting by vacuity. The intended claim needs a non-vacuous guard, not a patch to this result.
# This is a finite model of one reading; it makes no claim about any real history or any person.
